// Class 11 Economics practice bank, part 2: Index Numbers and Introductory Microeconomics (Ch 7-12).
StudyData.addPractice(11, "economics", {
  "index-numbers": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The price index of a city for the current year is 125 with the base year = 100. This means that, compared with the base year, prices have:", options: ["risen by 25%", "risen by 125%", "fallen by 25%", "risen by 12.5%"], answer: 0, explain: "The base year index is 100, so an index of 125 means a rise of 125 − 100 = 25%.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The price of a kilogram of apples was ₹40 in the base year and is ₹50 now. The price relative is:", options: ["80", "125", "110", "25"], answer: 1, explain: "Price relative = (p₁ ÷ p₀) × 100 = (50 ÷ 40) × 100 = 125.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Nifty 50 is the stock market index of the:", options: ["Bombay Stock Exchange", "Reserve Bank of India", "Securities and Exchange Board of India", "National Stock Exchange"], answer: 3, explain: "The Sensex belongs to the Bombay Stock Exchange (30 stocks); the Nifty 50 belongs to the National Stock Exchange.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The base year of the Sensex is:", options: ["1950–51", "1991–92", "1978–79", "2004–05"], answer: 2, explain: "The Sensex has the base 1978–79 = 100 and is based on 30 stocks.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Real wage is calculated as:", options: ["money wage × CPI ÷ 100", "CPI ÷ money wage × 100", "money wage − CPI", "money wage ÷ CPI × 100"], answer: 3, explain: "Real wage = (money wage ÷ CPI) × 100. It expresses the wage in base-year purchasing power.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Prices of three goods in the base year are ₹10, ₹20 and ₹30. In the current year they are ₹12, ₹24 and ₹36. The simple aggregative price index is:", options: ["110", "112", "120", "100"], answer: 2, explain: "Σp₀ = 10 + 20 + 30 = 60 and Σp₁ = 12 + 24 + 36 = 72. Index = (72 ÷ 60) × 100 = 120.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Two goods have base-year prices ₹20 and ₹50 and current prices ₹30 and ₹60. The simple average of price relatives is:", options: ["130", "135", "140", "125"], answer: 1, explain: "Price relatives: (30 ÷ 20) × 100 = 150 and (60 ÷ 50) × 100 = 120. Average = (150 + 120) ÷ 2 = 135.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Good A: p₀ = ₹5, q₀ = 4, p₁ = ₹6, q₁ = 2. Good B: p₀ = ₹10, q₀ = 2, p₁ = ₹11, q₁ = 4. Laspeyres' price index is:", options: ["115", "112", "113.5", "110"], answer: 0, explain: "Σp₁q₀ = 6 × 4 + 11 × 2 = 46 and Σp₀q₀ = 5 × 4 + 10 × 2 = 40. Laspeyres = (46 ÷ 40) × 100 = 115. (112 is Paasche's index for the same data.)", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The base year of an index number should be a normal year.\nReason (R): In an abnormal year, such as a drought or war year, prices are unusually high or low, so comparisons with it would be misleading.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "A normal base year gives a fair standard of comparison. Abnormal conditions distort prices, which is why they are avoided. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Asha's pocket money rose from ₹500 to ₹600 a month. During the same period the price index of the things she buys rose from 100 to 140. Which statement is correct?", options: ["Her pocket money has increased in real terms because ₹600 is more than ₹500", "Her pocket money has fallen in real terms because prices rose by 40% while her pocket money rose by only 20%", "Her real pocket money is unchanged", "Her real pocket money has risen by 40%"], answer: 1, explain: "Real pocket money = (600 ÷ 140) × 100 ≈ ₹428.6, which is less than ₹500. Prices rose faster than her money income.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A: p₀ = ₹8, p₁ = ₹10, q₀ = 6, q₁ = 5. B: p₀ = ₹5, p₁ = ₹6, q₀ = 8, q₁ = 10. Paasche's price index is approximately:", options: ["122.7", "122.2", "110", "120"], answer: 1, explain: "Σp₁q₁ = 10 × 5 + 6 × 10 = 110 and Σp₀q₁ = 8 × 5 + 5 × 10 = 90. Paasche = (110 ÷ 90) × 100 ≈ 122.2. (122.7 is Laspeyres: 108 ÷ 88.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A worker's money wage rises from ₹6,000 to ₹6,600 while the CPI rises from 120 to 150. His real wage:", options: ["rose by 10%", "fell by 12%", "fell by 10%", "did not change"], answer: 1, explain: "Real wage before = (6,000 ÷ 120) × 100 = ₹5,000. Real wage now = (6,600 ÷ 150) × 100 = ₹4,400. The fall is 600 ÷ 5,000 = 12%.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Laspeyres' index and Paasche's index always give the same value for the same data.\nReason (R): The two indices use different weights: base-year quantities and current-year quantities.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true: Laspeyres uses q₀ and Paasche uses q₁. Because the weights differ, the two values are normally different, so A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: In 2015 (base year, CPI = 100) a clerk earned ₹20,000 a month. By 2025 the CPI is 160. To keep his real wage the same as in 2015, his money wage in 2025 must be:", options: ["₹36,000", "₹26,000", "₹32,000", "₹20,160"], answer: 2, explain: "Real wage = money wage ÷ CPI × 100. To keep ₹20,000 of real wage at CPI 160, money wage = 20,000 × 160 ÷ 100 = ₹32,000.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define a price relative.", a: "A price relative is the current price of a good expressed as a percentage of its base-year price: (p₁ ÷ p₀) × 100.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Which index is also called the cost of living index?", a: "The Consumer Price Index (CPI), because it measures the change in retail prices of the goods and services a group of consumers buys.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State any two problems faced in constructing an index number.", a: "1. **Choice of base year**: it must be a normal year, not too far in the past.\n2. **Selection of items and weights**, and the reliability of price data: the items must represent what people actually buy, and the weights must show their relative importance.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The CPI rises from 125 to 140. Calculate the rate of inflation.", a: "Inflation rate = ((140 − 125) ÷ 125) × 100\n= (15 ÷ 125) × 100\n= **12%**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Prices of rice, milk and oil were ₹40, ₹50 and ₹120 in the base year and are ₹48, ₹65 and ₹126 now. Find the simple average of price relatives.", a: "Price relatives: rice (48 ÷ 40) × 100 = 120; milk (65 ÷ 50) × 100 = 130; oil (126 ÷ 120) × 100 = 105.\nSum = 120 + 130 + 105 = 355.\nIndex = 355 ÷ 3 ≈ **118.33**, so prices rose by about 18.3%.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A worker earned ₹18,000 when the CPI was 120. A year later he earns ₹21,000 when the CPI is 150. Calculate his real wage in both years and say whether he is better off.", a: "Real wage in year 1 = (18,000 ÷ 120) × 100 = ₹15,000.\nReal wage in year 2 = (21,000 ÷ 150) × 100 = ₹14,000.\nAlthough the money wage rose, the real wage **fell by ₹1,000**, so he is worse off: prices rose by 25% while his money wage rose only 16.7%.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain why a simple index number is considered less reliable than a weighted index number.", a: "1. A simple index gives **equal importance** to every item, although rice matters far more than salt in a household's budget.\n2. A weighted index uses **weights** (quantities or expenditure shares) so that important goods have a bigger effect on the index.\n3. The simple aggregative index is also affected by the **units** in which prices are quoted, whereas weights make the weighted index a truer picture of the change in the cost of living.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "From the data below, calculate Laspeyres' and Paasche's price indices.\nGood: p₀ (₹), q₀, p₁ (₹), q₁\nRice: 30, 20, 36, 18\nDal: 80, 5, 100, 6\nOil: 100, 4, 150, 3\nSugar: 40, 8, 44, 10\nWhy do the two values differ?", a: "Laspeyres (weights q₀):\nΣp₁q₀ = 36×20 + 100×5 + 150×4 + 44×8 = 720 + 500 + 600 + 352 = 2,172\nΣp₀q₀ = 30×20 + 80×5 + 100×4 + 40×8 = 600 + 400 + 400 + 320 = 1,720\nLaspeyres = (2,172 ÷ 1,720) × 100 ≈ **126.28**\n\nPaasche (weights q₁):\nΣp₁q₁ = 36×18 + 100×6 + 150×3 + 44×10 = 648 + 600 + 450 + 440 = 2,138\nΣp₀q₁ = 30×18 + 80×6 + 100×3 + 40×10 = 540 + 480 + 300 + 400 = 1,720\nPaasche = (2,138 ÷ 1,720) × 100 ≈ **124.30**\n\nThe values differ because Laspeyres uses base-year quantities and Paasche uses current-year quantities. Here consumers bought less of the goods whose prices rose most (oil), so Paasche is lower.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain five problems or precautions in constructing a price index number.", a: "1. **Base year**: it should be a normal year (no drought, flood or war) and not too distant, so that comparisons are meaningful.\n2. **Selection of items**: the basket must include the goods and services that the group of consumers actually buys.\n3. **Weights**: items must be given weights according to their relative importance, for example their share in expenditure.\n4. **Price data**: prices must be collected reliably and from suitable markets: retail prices for a CPI, wholesale prices for a WPI.\n5. **Choice of formula**: simple or weighted, Laspeyres or Paasche, depending on the purpose and the data available.\nThe index is only as good as these choices.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A town has CPI = 125 in 2022 and CPI = 140 in 2023. A clerk's monthly wage was ₹25,000 in 2022 and ₹27,300 in 2023.\n(i) Find the inflation rate in 2023.\n(ii) Find his real wage in both years and say whether it rose or fell.\n(iii) How much money wage would keep his real wage at the 2022 level?", a: "(i) Inflation = ((140 − 125) ÷ 125) × 100 = **12%**.\n(ii) Real wage 2022 = (25,000 ÷ 125) × 100 = ₹20,000. Real wage 2023 = (27,300 ÷ 140) × 100 = ₹19,500. The real wage **fell by ₹500** (2.5%), because the money wage rose only 9.2% against 12% inflation.\n(iii) Required wage = 25,000 × 140 ÷ 125 = **₹28,000**, i.e. a further ₹700 as dearness allowance.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "micro-introduction": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Every society has to make choices about what to produce mainly because:", options: ["money is not available", "resources are scarce and have alternative uses", "the government controls production", "people do not want many goods"], answer: 1, explain: "Resources are limited relative to wants and can be used in different ways. Scarcity with alternative uses forces choice.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is NOT a central problem of an economy?", options: ["What to produce", "For whom to produce", "How to print currency notes", "How to produce"], answer: 2, explain: "The three central problems are what to produce, how to produce and for whom to produce. Printing currency is a monetary policy matter, not a central problem.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The opportunity cost of a choice is:", options: ["the value of the next best alternative given up", "the money spent on the chosen good", "the total of all alternatives given up", "the cost of production only"], answer: 0, explain: "Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone, not of all the alternatives and not necessarily a money payment.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A production possibility frontier shows:", options: ["the prices of two goods", "the minimum output that a country can produce", "the profit of a producer", "the combinations of two goods that can be produced with fully and efficiently used resources"], answer: 3, explain: "The PPF is the boundary of the production possibility set: the maximum combinations obtainable from given resources and technology.", level: 1 },
      { q: "India is best described as a:", options: ["centrally planned economy", "mixed economy", "pure market economy", "subsistence economy"], answer: 1, explain: "In India the market decides most things, but the government also plays an important role, so it is a mixed economy.", level: 1 },

      { q: "When an economy increases the output of good X from 4 to 6 units, the output of good Y falls from 18 to 12 units. The MRT is:", options: ["6", "2", "3", "12"], answer: 2, explain: "MRT = ΔY ÷ ΔX = (18 − 12) ÷ (6 − 4) = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 units of Y per extra unit of X.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: A severe earthquake destroys many factories and roads in a region. On the region's PPF diagram this will be shown as:", options: ["an inward shift of the PPF", "an outward shift of the PPF", "a movement along the PPF", "a movement from a point on the PPF to a point outside it"], answer: 0, explain: "The earthquake reduces the productive capacity (capital and infrastructure), so the maximum output of the goods falls and the PPF shifts inward.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a positive statement?", options: ["The government should provide free healthcare", "Rich people ought to pay higher taxes", "Unemployment is the worst problem facing the economy", "The unemployment rate in the economy was 6% last year"], answer: 3, explain: "A positive statement describes what is and can be checked against facts. 'Should', 'ought' and 'worst' express value judgements, which are normative.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A production possibility frontier slopes downward.\nReason (R): With given resources, producing more of one good requires moving resources away from the other good.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Resources are limited, so more of one good means less of the other. This gives the negative slope. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A PPF is a straight line. This means that:", options: ["the economy is using its resources inefficiently", "the marginal rate of transformation is constant", "the economy produces only one good", "the opportunity cost keeps rising"], answer: 1, explain: "A straight line has a constant slope, so the MRT (opportunity cost) is constant. This is the case when resources are equally efficient in producing both goods.", level: 2 },

      { q: "An economy's production possibilities are: X = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and Y = 40, 36, 30, 22, 12. The total quantity of Y given up to raise X from 0 to 3 units is:", options: ["8", "18", "22", "10"], answer: 1, explain: "Y falls from 40 to 22, so 18 units are given up (MRT of 4, 6 and 8 for the three units: 4 + 6 + 8 = 18). The MRT rises, so the PPF is concave.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): When many workers become unemployed, the economy moves to a point inside its PPF.\nReason (R): The PPF shifts inward whenever workers become unemployed.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Idle labour means under-utilisation, so the economy produces at a point inside the PPF (A is true). The PPF itself depends on available resources and technology and does not shift because of unemployment, so R is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A farmer can use his whole plot to grow either 60 quintals of wheat or 40 quintals of gram. Assume that the land can be divided and that the opportunity cost stays constant. The opportunity cost of growing 10 extra quintals of gram is:", options: ["10 quintals of wheat", "15 quintals of wheat", "6.67 quintals of wheat", "25 quintals of wheat"], answer: 1, explain: "Giving up 60 quintals of wheat gives 40 quintals of gram, so 1 quintal of gram costs 60 ÷ 40 = 1.5 quintals of wheat. For 10 quintals: 15 quintals of wheat.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Rohan can earn ₹12,000 a month from a job, or ₹9,000 a month from home tuition, or he can study full time for a course. He chooses to study full time, which means he cannot do either work option. His monthly opportunity cost of studying (in income given up) is:", options: ["₹21,000", "₹9,000", "₹3,000", "₹12,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative given up. The best of the work alternatives is the job at ₹12,000. The alternatives are not added because he could do only one of them.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define scarcity.", a: "Scarcity is the situation in which resources are limited in relation to human wants and have alternative uses, so choices have to be made.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the marginal rate of transformation (MRT)?", a: "MRT is the number of units of one good that must be given up to produce one more unit of the other good (ΔY ÷ ΔX). It is the slope of the PPF in absolute value.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between positive and normative economics with one example of each.", a: "1. **Positive economics** studies how the economy works, i.e. what is. Example: 'Inflation was 5% last year.'\n2. **Normative economics** judges what ought to be. Example: 'The government should control inflation at any cost.'", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why does a production possibility frontier slope downward?", a: "1. Resources and technology are given, so the economy has a limited capacity.\n2. To produce more of one good, resources must be moved from the other good, so its output must fall. Hence the PPF has a negative slope.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "An economy's production possibilities are: X = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and Y = 50, 47, 42, 35, 26. Calculate the MRT for each additional unit of X and state the shape of the PPF.", a: "MRT = ΔY ÷ ΔX:\n1st unit of X: 50 − 47 = 3\n2nd unit: 47 − 42 = 5\n3rd unit: 42 − 35 = 7\n4th unit: 35 − 26 = 9\nMRT = 3, 5, 7, 9. It is rising, so the PPF is **concave to the origin**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how each of the following is shown on a PPF diagram: (a) mass unemployment, (b) discovery of better technology, (c) destruction of capital by a flood.", a: "(a) Unemployment means under-utilised resources, so the economy is at a point **inside** the PPF.\n(b) Better technology raises the maximum output of the goods, so the PPF **shifts outward**.\n(c) Destruction of capital reduces productive capacity, so the PPF **shifts inward**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Distinguish between microeconomics and macroeconomics on any three points.", a: "1. **Subject matter**: micro studies individual units (a consumer, a firm, one market); macro studies the economy as a whole.\n2. **Variables**: micro deals with the price and output of a single good; macro deals with aggregates such as national income, general price level and employment.\n3. **Examples**: determination of the price of wheat (micro); causes of inflation or unemployment in the whole economy (macro).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Distinguish between a centrally planned economy and a market economy. How is India different from both?", a: "1. In a **centrally planned economy** the government or a central authority decides what, how and for whom to produce.\n2. In a **market economy** these decisions are made by buyers and sellers through the market, and prices coordinate them (the price mechanism).\n3. In a planned economy prices and output are fixed by the plan; in a market economy they respond to demand and supply.\n4. India is a **mixed economy**: private firms and markets decide most activities.\n5. The government also plays a major role, for example by providing public goods and regulating markets.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "An economy's production possibilities are: X = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and Y = 20, 19, 17, 14, 10.\n(a) Calculate the MRT for each unit of X.\n(b) State the shape of the PPF with a reason.\n(c) What does the combination (X = 3, Y = 16) show? What does (X = 3, Y = 10) show?", a: "(a) MRT = ΔY ÷ ΔX: 20 − 19 = 1; 19 − 17 = 2; 17 − 14 = 3; 14 − 10 = 4. MRT = **1, 2, 3, 4**.\n(b) The MRT is rising, so the opportunity cost of each extra unit of X is higher: the PPF is **concave to the origin**. This is because resources are not equally efficient in producing both goods.\n(c) At X = 3 the maximum Y is 14. The combination (3, 16) needs more Y than the PPF allows, so it lies **outside** the PPF and is **unattainable** with the given resources.\nSimilarly, (3, 10) lies **inside** the PPF: the economy can produce 4 more units of Y at the same X, so resources are under-utilised or used inefficiently.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: An island produces coconuts and fish using the same labour. Its production possibilities are:\nFish (tonnes): 0, 10, 20, 30\nCoconuts (thousands): 60, 54, 42, 24\n(i) What is the opportunity cost of the first 10 tonnes of fish and of the third 10 tonnes?\n(ii) What is the shape of the PPF? Give a reason.\n(iii) A storm destroys many coconut trees. What happens to the PPF?", a: "(i) First 10 tonnes of fish: 60 − 54 = **6 thousand coconuts**. Third 10 tonnes: 42 − 24 = **18 thousand coconuts**.\n(ii) The opportunity cost rises (6, 12, 18), so the PPF is **concave to the origin**; workers are not equally good at fishing and gathering coconuts.\n(iii) The storm reduces productive capacity, so the maximum coconut output falls and the PPF **shifts inward** (at least at the coconut end).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "theory-of-consumer-behaviour": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Marginal utility is:", options: ["the total satisfaction from all units consumed", "the satisfaction from the first unit only", "the addition to total utility from consuming one more unit", "the average satisfaction per unit"], answer: 2, explain: "MU = ΔTU ÷ ΔQ: the change in total utility caused by consuming one more unit. Total utility is the sum of all MUs.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Total utility is maximum when marginal utility is:", options: ["zero", "maximum", "negative", "equal to price"], answer: 0, explain: "TU rises while MU is positive, stops rising when MU = 0 and falls when MU becomes negative. So TU is maximum at MU = 0.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The slope of the budget line p₁x₁ + p₂x₂ = M (with good 1 on the horizontal axis) in absolute value is:", options: ["M ÷ p₁", "M ÷ p₂", "p₂ ÷ p₁", "p₁ ÷ p₂"], answer: 3, explain: "The intercepts are M ÷ p₁ and M ÷ p₂, so the slope in absolute value is (M ÷ p₂) ÷ (M ÷ p₁) = p₁ ÷ p₂.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Two indifference curves of the same consumer cannot intersect because:", options: ["they are always parallel", "that would give two different levels of satisfaction for the same bundle", "indifference curves are straight lines", "income is fixed"], answer: 1, explain: "At the point of intersection one bundle would lie on two curves with different satisfaction levels, which contradicts the definition of an indifference curve.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A good whose demand falls when the consumer's income rises is called a(n):", options: ["inferior good", "normal good", "substitute good", "complementary good"], answer: 0, explain: "Demand for a normal good rises with income; for an inferior good (such as coarse cereals) it falls.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A consumer's marginal utility (in rupees) from successive units of a good is ₹10, ₹8, ₹6, ₹4 and ₹2. The price of the good is ₹6. How many units will he buy to be in equilibrium?", options: ["2", "3", "4", "5"], answer: 1, explain: "Equilibrium is where MU = price. MU of the 3rd unit is ₹6, equal to the price. He buys 3 units. A 4th unit would add only ₹4 for a cost of ₹6.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The demand function is q = 80 − 2p. The price elasticity of demand (in absolute value) at the price p = ₹30 is:", options: ["1", "2", "3", "1/3"], answer: 2, explain: "At p = 30, q = 80 − 60 = 20. |eD| = (−dq/dp) × p ÷ q = 2 × 30 ÷ 20 = 3. Geometrically: lower segment ÷ upper segment = 60 ÷ 20 = 3.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The price of a good rises from ₹20 to ₹24 and the quantity demanded falls from 100 to 85 units. Using the percentage method, the price elasticity of demand (absolute value) is:", options: ["0.75", "1.33", "1.5", "0.5"], answer: 0, explain: "% change in price = 4 ÷ 20 = 20%. % change in quantity = 15 ÷ 100 = 15%. |eD| = 15 ÷ 20 = 0.75, which is less than 1, so demand is inelastic.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A rise in the price of tea increases the demand for coffee.\nReason (R): Tea and coffee are substitutes.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "When tea becomes dearer, consumers switch to its close substitute, coffee, so its demand curve shifts right. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Sunita has ₹180 to spend on pens (₹6 each, good 1) and notebooks (₹12 each, good 2). At her present bundle the MRS is 3 (she would give up 3 notebooks for one more pen). What should she do?", options: ["Stay where she is; she is in equilibrium", "Buy more pens and fewer notebooks", "Buy more notebooks and fewer pens", "Spend less than ₹180"], answer: 1, explain: "The price ratio p₁ ÷ p₂ = 6 ÷ 12 = 0.5. Since MRS (3) > 0.5, she values a pen more than the market asks. She gains by buying more pens and fewer notebooks until MRS falls to 0.5.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A consumer has MU of good X = 20 utils at price ₹4 and MU of good Y = 8 utils at price ₹2. To raise total utility within the same budget he should:", options: ["buy more X and less Y", "buy more Y and less X", "buy more of both", "make no change"], answer: 0, explain: "MUx ÷ Px = 20 ÷ 4 = 5 utils per rupee; MUy ÷ Py = 8 ÷ 2 = 4. A rupee gives more utility on X, so shift spending to X until the ratios are equal (diminishing MU brings this about).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A consumer's income is ₹240 and the prices of goods 1 and 2 are ₹12 and ₹8. The price of good 1 falls to ₹8. The new intercept of the budget line on the good 1 axis and the new slope (absolute value) are:", options: ["20 units and 1.5", "30 units and 1.5", "30 units and 1", "20 units and 1"], answer: 2, explain: "New intercept on the good 1 axis = 240 ÷ 8 = 30 (it was 20). The good 2 intercept 240 ÷ 8 = 30 is unchanged. New slope = 8 ÷ 8 = 1 (it was 12 ÷ 8 = 1.5): the line becomes flatter.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A bus operator raises the fare from ₹20 to ₹25 and the daily number of passengers falls from 800 to 700. Which statement is correct?", options: ["|eD| = 1.25 and daily revenue falls", "|eD| = 0.5 and daily revenue rises from ₹16,000 to ₹17,500", "|eD| = 2 and daily revenue rises", "|eD| = 0.5 and daily revenue falls"], answer: 1, explain: "% change in price = 5 ÷ 20 = 25%; % change in quantity = 100 ÷ 800 = 12.5%. |eD| = 12.5 ÷ 25 = 0.5, so demand is inelastic and revenue rises: 20 × 800 = ₹16,000 to 25 × 700 = ₹17,500.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): At the consumer's optimum the indifference curve crosses the budget line.\nReason (R): A consumer prefers a higher indifference curve to a lower one.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "At the optimum the IC is tangent to the budget line (MRS = p₁ ÷ p₂); if it crossed the line, a higher IC would still be reachable. So A is false. R is a true statement about preferences.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define the marginal rate of substitution (MRS).", a: "MRS is the amount of good 2 that a consumer is willing to give up to get one more unit of good 1 while staying at the same level of satisfaction. It is the absolute slope of the indifference curve.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What are complementary goods? Give an example.", a: "Complementary goods are goods used together, so a rise in the price of one lowers the demand for the other. Example: tea and sugar (or petrol and cars).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A consumer's total utility from 1 to 6 units of a good is 12, 22, 30, 36, 40, 40. Find the marginal utility of each unit and say at which unit it becomes zero.", a: "MU = change in TU:\n1st = 12; 2nd = 22 − 12 = 10; 3rd = 30 − 22 = 8; 4th = 36 − 30 = 6; 5th = 40 − 36 = 4; 6th = 40 − 40 = 0.\nMU becomes **zero at the 6th unit**, where TU stops rising and is maximum (40). MU keeps falling, which illustrates the law of diminishing marginal utility.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between a normal good and an inferior good with an example of each.", a: "1. For a **normal good** demand rises when income rises (example: milk or fruit).\n2. For an **inferior good** demand falls when income rises (example: coarse cereals), because consumers switch to better alternatives.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A consumer has an income of ₹60 and buys good 1 at ₹5 and good 2 at ₹10. (a) Write the budget line. (b) Find the maximum quantity of each good. (c) Find the slope.", a: "(a) Budget line: 5x₁ + 10x₂ = 60.\n(b) Maximum of good 1 = 60 ÷ 5 = 12 units; maximum of good 2 = 60 ÷ 10 = 6 units.\n(c) Slope = −p₁ ÷ p₂ = −5 ÷ 10 = **−0.5** (also 6 ÷ 12).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The price of a good rises from ₹80 to ₹90 and its quantity demanded falls from 400 to 340 units. Find the price elasticity of demand, state the type of demand, and verify the effect on total expenditure.", a: "% change in price = (10 ÷ 80) × 100 = 12.5%.\n% change in quantity = (−60 ÷ 400) × 100 = −15%.\neD = −15 ÷ 12.5 = **−1.2**, so |eD| = 1.2 > 1: demand is **elastic**.\nCheck: expenditure falls from 80 × 400 = ₹32,000 to 90 × 340 = ₹30,600. For elastic demand, a rise in price lowers total expenditure.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The demand function is q = 100 − 4p. Find the price elasticity of demand at p = ₹10 by the formula method and by the geometric method.", a: "At p = 10, q = 100 − 40 = 60.\n**Formula**: |eD| = (dq/dp) × p ÷ q = 4 × 10 ÷ 60 = **2/3**.\n**Geometric**: the demand line meets the quantity axis at q = 100 (p = 0). The point q = 60 divides the line into an upper segment (towards the price axis) proportional to 60 and a lower segment proportional to 100 − 60 = 40.\n|eD| = lower segment ÷ upper segment = 40 ÷ 60 = **2/3**.\nBoth methods agree: demand is inelastic at this point.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A consumer spends his income on goods X and Y, with Px = ₹2, Py = ₹1 and income ₹10. The marginal utilities (in utils) from successive units are:\nUnit: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\nMUx: 16, 14, 12, 10, 8\nMUy: 9, 8, 7, 6, 5\nFind the units of X and Y at equilibrium using the utility approach and the total utility obtained.", a: "Compute MU per rupee:\nMUx ÷ Px: 8, 7, 6, 5, 4\nMUy ÷ Py: 9, 8, 7, 6, 5\nThe consumer buys the units with the highest MU per rupee while income lasts.\nEquilibrium needs MUx ÷ Px = MUy ÷ Py. This holds at the value **6**, when X = 3 units and Y = 4 units.\nExpenditure = 3 × 2 + 4 × 1 = 6 + 4 = ₹10 = income, so the budget is exhausted.\nTotal utility = (16 + 14 + 12) + (9 + 8 + 7 + 6) = 42 + 30 = **72 utils**.\nCheck: the last X bought (3rd) and the last Y bought (4th) both give 6 utils per rupee. The next units, the 4th X and the 5th Y, give only 5 per rupee, so no change in the bundle can raise total utility.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the properties of indifference curves.", a: "An indifference curve (IC) joins all bundles of two goods that give the same satisfaction; a set of ICs is an indifference map. Its properties:\n1. **Downward sloping**: with monotonic preferences, more of one good must be offset by less of the other to keep satisfaction constant.\n2. **Convex to the origin**: because the MRS diminishes as the consumer gets more of good 1, the consumer gives up less and less of good 2 for each extra unit.\n3. **Higher IC means higher satisfaction**: a higher curve has bundles with more of at least one good.\n4. **Two ICs never intersect**: the common bundle would give two different levels of satisfaction, which is impossible.\n5. The slope at any point is the MRS, which is used in the equilibrium condition MRS = p₁ ÷ p₂.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A juice stall sells glasses of sugarcane juice. When the price is ₹10, it sells 50 glasses a day. When the price falls to ₹8, it sells 70 glasses. When the price falls further to ₹6, it sells 84 glasses.\n(i) Find the price elasticity of demand between ₹10 and ₹8.\n(ii) Find the price elasticity between ₹8 and ₹6 and state whether demand is elastic or inelastic.\n(iii) State two factors that make demand for a good more elastic.", a: "(i) % change in price = −2 ÷ 10 = −20%; % change in quantity = 20 ÷ 50 = 40%. eD = 40 ÷ (−20) = **−2**, so |eD| = 2: elastic. Expenditure rises from ₹500 to ₹560.\n(ii) % change in price = −2 ÷ 8 = −25%; % change in quantity = 14 ÷ 70 = 20%. eD = 20 ÷ (−25) = **−0.8**, so |eD| = 0.8: inelastic. Expenditure falls from ₹560 to ₹504.\n(iii) Demand is more elastic when the good is a **luxury** rather than a necessity, and when it has **many close substitutes** (for example other cold drinks).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "production-and-costs": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A production function shows the relationship between:", options: ["price and quantity supplied", "cost and revenue", "profit and output", "the maximum output and the quantities of inputs used"], answer: 3, explain: "The production function q = f(L, K) gives the maximum output obtainable from given quantities of inputs with the given technology.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In the short run:", options: ["all inputs are variable", "at least one input is fixed", "all inputs are fixed", "there are no costs"], answer: 1, explain: "The short run is the period in which at least one input (usually capital) cannot be changed. When all inputs can be varied it is the long run.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following does NOT change as output increases in the short run?", options: ["Total fixed cost", "Total variable cost", "Marginal cost", "Average variable cost"], answer: 0, explain: "TFC is the cost of fixed inputs and is the same at every output level, even zero. TVC, MC and AVC all depend on output.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Marginal cost is calculated as:", options: ["TC ÷ q", "TVC ÷ q", "ΔTC ÷ Δq", "TFC ÷ q"], answer: 2, explain: "MC is the addition to total cost when output rises by one unit: MC = ΔTC ÷ Δq (which also equals ΔTVC ÷ Δq).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Average product is calculated as:", options: ["ΔTP ÷ ΔL", "TP ÷ L", "TP × L", "L ÷ TP"], answer: 1, explain: "AP = TP ÷ L. MP = ΔTP ÷ ΔL is the marginal product.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Total product for 1 to 6 workers is 6, 18, 36, 52, 62 and 66 units. Diminishing marginal product begins from the:", options: ["3rd worker", "4th worker", "5th worker", "6th worker"], answer: 1, explain: "MP = 6, 12, 18, 16, 10, 4. MP rises up to the 3rd worker (18) and starts falling from the 4th (16).", level: 2 },
      { q: "A firm's TFC is ₹50. Its TC at 1, 2, 3 and 4 units is ₹80, ₹100, ₹126 and ₹160. The marginal cost of the 3rd unit is:", options: ["₹42", "₹76", "₹26", "₹25.33"], answer: 2, explain: "MC₃ = TC₃ − TC₂ = 126 − 100 = ₹26. (₹42 is the average cost, ₹76 is TVC and ₹25.33 is AVC.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "A firm has the production function q = L^0.4 K^0.3. If all inputs are doubled, output will:", options: ["more than double", "exactly double", "less than double", "stay the same"], answer: 2, explain: "The sum of exponents is 0.4 + 0.3 = 0.7, which is less than 1, so there are decreasing returns to scale and output rises by less than a factor of 2.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The average fixed cost falls continuously as output increases.\nReason (R): Total fixed cost remains constant while output rises.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "AFC = TFC ÷ q. TFC is a constant, so as q rises the AFC falls. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A bakery has one oven (fixed). With 1 to 5 bakers the daily output is 20, 50, 70, 80 and 80 loaves. The owner should not hire the 5th baker because:", options: ["the marginal product of the 5th baker is zero", "the average product is rising", "the marginal product of the 5th baker is higher than that of the 4th", "the total product has fallen"], answer: 0, explain: "MP of the 5th baker = 80 − 80 = 0. He adds nothing to output but adds to cost, so hiring him is not rational.", level: 2 },

      { q: "The TVC of producing 1 to 5 units is ₹12, ₹22, ₹30, ₹42 and ₹60. The AVC is minimum at:", options: ["2 units, at ₹11", "3 units, at ₹10", "4 units, at ₹10.50", "1 unit, at ₹12"], answer: 1, explain: "AVC = 12, 11, 10, 10.5, 12. The minimum is ₹10 at 3 units. MC = 12, 10, 8, 12, 18; MC (₹12) exceeds AVC (₹10) at the 4th unit, so AVC rises from there.", level: 3 },
      { q: "At 8 units of output a firm's AFC is ₹5 and its AVC is ₹20. Its total cost at 7 units is ₹177. The marginal cost of the 8th unit is:", options: ["₹25", "₹20", "₹3", "₹23"], answer: 3, explain: "TFC = 5 × 8 = ₹40 and TVC = 20 × 8 = ₹160, so TC₈ = ₹200. MC₈ = 200 − 177 = ₹23.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The gap between the AC curve and the AVC curve goes on increasing as output rises.\nReason (R): The AFC falls as output rises.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "AC − AVC = AFC, which falls as output rises, so the gap narrows (but never disappears because AFC is always positive). A is false and R is true.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A factory employs 10 workers and 5 machines and produces 400 units. After doubling to 20 workers and 10 machines it produces 700 units. This shows:", options: ["increasing returns to scale", "constant returns to scale", "decreasing returns to scale", "diminishing returns to a factor"], answer: 2, explain: "Both inputs were doubled (a change in scale) but output rose only 1.75 times (700 ÷ 400), a smaller proportion. This is decreasing returns to scale; returns to a factor involve changing only one input.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define the production function.", a: "The production function is the technical relationship that gives the maximum output obtainable from given quantities of inputs, with the given technology: q = f(L, K).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "How is total variable cost related to marginal cost?", a: "TVC is the sum of the marginal costs of all units produced: TVC = ΣMC. (Equivalently, MC = ΔTVC ÷ Δq.)", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the short run and the long run in production.", a: "1. In the **short run** at least one input is fixed, so output can be changed only by changing the variable inputs; fixed costs exist.\n2. In the **long run** all inputs are variable, so there are no fixed costs and the scale of production can be changed.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Total product is 18 units with 2 workers and 30 units with 3 workers. Find the marginal product of the 3rd worker and the average product with 3 workers. Does AP rise or fall when the third worker is hired?", a: "MP₃ = 30 − 18 = **12 units**.\nAP₃ = 30 ÷ 3 = **10 units**; AP₂ = 18 ÷ 2 = 9 units.\nSince MP₃ (12) > AP₂ (9), the average product **rises** to 10.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the shapes of the TFC, TVC and TC curves in the short run and the relation between them.", a: "1. **TFC** is a horizontal straight line, because fixed cost does not change with output.\n2. **TVC** starts from the origin and rises, first at a decreasing rate and then at an increasing rate (law of variable proportions).\n3. **TC = TFC + TVC**, so the TC curve has the same shape as TVC and starts from the TFC level on the vertical axis; the vertical distance between TC and TVC equals TFC at every output.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Classify each production function as showing increasing, constant or decreasing returns to scale, and verify the first one by doubling the inputs.\n(a) q = L^0.5 K^0.5\n(b) q = L^0.4 K^0.4\n(c) q = L^0.7 K^0.6", a: "For q = L^α K^β, returns to scale are constant if α + β = 1, increasing if α + β > 1, decreasing if α + β < 1.\n(a) 0.5 + 0.5 = 1: **constant returns to scale**. Check: (2L)^0.5 (2K)^0.5 = 2^(0.5 + 0.5) L^0.5 K^0.5 = 2q: output exactly doubles.\n(b) 0.4 + 0.4 = 0.8 < 1: **decreasing returns to scale**.\n(c) 0.7 + 0.6 = 1.3 > 1: **increasing returns to scale**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain why the short-run average cost curve is U-shaped.", a: "1. AC = AFC + AVC.\n2. At the start output is low, so AFC falls rapidly, and AVC also falls (MP of the variable input is rising), so AC falls.\n3. Later, because of the law of variable proportions, AVC begins to rise and eventually rises faster than AFC falls, so AC rises. Hence AC is U-shaped, and MC cuts it at its minimum.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A firm has TFC = ₹40 and TVC of ₹20, ₹36, ₹48, ₹66 and ₹92 for 1 to 5 units. Prepare a table of TC, AFC, AVC, AC and MC.", a: "TC = TFC + TVC: 60, 76, 88, 106, 132.\nAFC = 40 ÷ q: 40, 20, 13.33, 10, 8.\nAVC = TVC ÷ q: 20, 18, 16, 16.50, 18.40.\nAC = TC ÷ q: 60, 38, 29.33, 26.50, 26.40. (Check: AC = AFC + AVC, e.g. 10 + 16.50 = 26.50.)\nMC = change in TC (TC at zero output = ₹40): 20, 16, 12, 18, 26. (Check: sum of MC = 92 = TVC at 5 units.)\n(All in ₹.) AVC is lowest at 3 units (₹16); from the 4th unit MC (₹18) is above AVC and AVC rises.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "The following table gives the total product of labour with fixed capital.\nL: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\nTP: 10, 24, 40, 52, 60, 64, 64\n(a) Calculate MP and AP.\n(b) Identify the three phases of the law of variable proportions.\n(c) In which phase will a rational producer operate? Why?", a: "(a) MP = change in TP: 10, 14, 16, 12, 8, 4, 0.\nAP = TP ÷ L: 10, 12, 13.33, 13, 12, 10.67, 9.14.\n(b) **Phase I** (1 to 3 workers): MP rises up to 16 (3rd worker) and TP rises at an increasing rate; AP reaches its maximum (13.33) at the 3rd worker.\n**Phase II** (4th to 7th worker): MP falls but stays non-negative; TP rises at a decreasing rate and becomes maximum (64) when MP = 0 at the 7th worker.\n**Phase III** (beyond 7 workers): MP would be negative and TP would fall.\n(c) A rational producer operates in **Phase II**: in Phase I an extra worker adds more than the average, so it pays to hire more, and in Phase III extra workers reduce output.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A garment unit has fixed machines and a building for which it pays TFC of ₹30,000 a month. Its variable costs (cloth, wages) are ₹50,000 for producing 1,000 shirts and ₹90,000 for producing 2,000 shirts.\n(i) Find TC and AC at 2,000 shirts.\n(ii) Find AFC and AVC at 1,000 shirts and at 2,000 shirts.\n(iii) Find the average marginal cost of the second 1,000 shirts. Is AVC falling or rising over this range?", a: "(i) TC at 2,000 = 30,000 + 90,000 = ₹1,20,000. AC = 1,20,000 ÷ 2,000 = **₹60** per shirt.\n(ii) At 1,000 shirts: AFC = 30,000 ÷ 1,000 = ₹30; AVC = 50,000 ÷ 1,000 = ₹50.\nAt 2,000 shirts: AFC = 30,000 ÷ 2,000 = ₹15; AVC = 90,000 ÷ 2,000 = ₹45.\n(iii) Extra cost = 90,000 − 50,000 = ₹40,000 for 1,000 shirts, so average MC = **₹40**. Since MC (₹40) is below AVC (₹50 at 1,000 shirts), AVC is **falling** (to ₹45).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "theory-of-firm-under-perfect-competition": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Under perfect competition, the demand curve faced by an individual firm is:", options: ["downward sloping", "a horizontal straight line at the market price", "a vertical straight line", "upward sloping"], answer: 1, explain: "The firm is a price-taker: it can sell any quantity at the market price and nothing at a higher price, so its demand curve is horizontal (perfectly elastic).", level: 1 },
      { q: "For a firm under perfect competition:", options: ["AR = MR = price", "MR is greater than AR", "AR is greater than MR", "MR is zero"], answer: 0, explain: "Every extra unit is sold at the same price, so each unit adds exactly the price to total revenue. Hence AR = MR = p.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A competitive firm earns only normal profit at the price that equals its:", options: ["minimum AVC", "maximum MC", "average fixed cost", "minimum average cost"], answer: 3, explain: "At p = min AC the total revenue equals total cost (the break-even point), so the firm earns only normal profit.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Profit of a firm is equal to:", options: ["TR + TC", "TC − TR", "TR − TC", "AR − MR"], answer: 2, explain: "Profit π = total revenue − total cost, where total cost includes normal profit.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The short-run supply curve of a competitive firm is the:", options: ["whole SMC curve", "rising part of the SMC curve at and above minimum AVC", "AVC curve", "rising part of the SMC curve at and above minimum AFC"], answer: 1, explain: "The firm supplies only when p is at least min AVC, and supplies the quantity at which p = rising SMC. So the supply curve is the rising portion of SMC from the minimum AVC upward.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A firm under perfect competition sells 40 units at ₹15 per unit. The marginal revenue from selling the 41st unit is:", options: ["₹10", "₹600", "₹615", "₹15"], answer: 3, explain: "For a price-taker MR = price = ₹15 at every output. (₹600 is the total revenue from 40 units.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "The market price is ₹18 and a firm's total costs are: 1 to 6 units ₹44, ₹54, ₹62, ₹72, ₹86, ₹106. TFC is ₹30. The profit-maximising output is:", options: ["4 units", "5 units", "6 units", "3 units"], answer: 1, explain: "MC of the units: 14, 10, 8, 10, 14, 20. The 5th unit adds ₹18 to revenue and ₹14 to cost, but the 6th adds ₹18 and ₹20. So the firm stops at 5 units, where profit = 90 − 86 = ₹4.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A firm has TFC = ₹500. At a price of ₹14 it can produce 100 units with an AVC of ₹16. The firm should:", options: ["stop production, because producing would lose more than ₹500", "produce, because it earns a profit", "produce, because TFC is a sunk cost", "produce, because price exceeds MC"], answer: 0, explain: "If it produces, TR = 1,400 and TC = 1,600 + 500 = 2,100, a loss of ₹700. If it shuts down it loses only TFC (₹500). Since price is below AVC, shutting down is better.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): For a firm under perfect competition, the AR curve and the MR curve coincide.\nReason (R): A price-taking firm sells every additional unit at the same price.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Because the price does not change with the quantity sold, MR equals the price, which is also the AR. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A potter's marginal cost of producing the 100th pot is ₹12. The government now imposes a unit tax of ₹2 on every pot sold. To be willing to supply 100 pots, the potter now needs a price of at least:", options: ["₹10", "₹12", "₹14", "₹24"], answer: 2, explain: "A unit tax raises MC by the amount of the tax. The firm supplies where p = MC, so the price needed for 100 pots rises from ₹12 to 12 + 2 = ₹14. The supply curve shifts upward (left).", level: 2 },

      { q: "A straight-line supply curve passes through the origin. At a price of ₹20 the quantity supplied is 80 units. If the price rises to ₹26, the quantity supplied will be:", options: ["100 units", "104 units", "90 units", "112 units"], answer: 1, explain: "For a straight line through the origin eS = 1, so quantity is proportional to price. Price rises by 30%, so quantity rises by 30%: 80 × 1.3 = 104 units.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A competitive firm has TVC = q² + 4q and TFC = ₹50. The market price is ₹24. The profit-maximising output and the profit are:", options: ["q = 10, profit ₹90", "q = 12, profit ₹46", "q = 5, profit ₹25", "q = 10, profit ₹50"], answer: 3, explain: "MC = 2q + 4. Setting p = MC: 24 = 2q + 4, so q = 10. TR = 240; TC = 100 + 40 + 50 = 190. Profit = ₹50. (₹90 forgets the fixed cost.)", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A competitive firm will never produce when the price is below its minimum average cost.\nReason (R): At such a price the firm makes a loss.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true: if p < min AC the firm makes a loss. But in the short run the firm continues to produce as long as p ≥ min AVC, because it then covers all its variable cost and part of its fixed cost. So A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: In a competitive market a firm sells 50 units at a price of ₹40. At this output its marginal cost is ₹40 (rising), AVC is ₹28 and AC is ₹35. The firm's profit is:", options: ["₹600", "₹2,000", "₹250", "₹5"], answer: 2, explain: "Profit = (price − AC) × q = (40 − 35) × 50 = ₹250. It is maximised because p = MC with MC rising. (₹600 = (40 − 28) × 50 is only the contribution over variable cost.)", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define marginal revenue.", a: "Marginal revenue is the addition to total revenue from selling one more unit of output: MR = ΔTR ÷ Δq. Under perfect competition MR = price.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the break-even point of a firm?", a: "It is the point where price equals the minimum average cost. Total revenue equals total cost and the firm earns only normal profit.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why is the demand curve faced by a firm under perfect competition perfectly elastic?", a: "1. There are many sellers of a homogeneous product, so no single firm can influence the market price; it can sell any quantity at that price.\n2. If it charges a higher price it loses all its buyers, and there is no need to charge less. Hence its demand curve is horizontal at the market price.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The price of a good rises from ₹15 to ₹18 and the quantity supplied rises from 50 to 56 units. Calculate the price elasticity of supply.", a: "% change in price = (3 ÷ 15) × 100 = 20%.\n% change in quantity = (6 ÷ 50) × 100 = 12%.\neS = 12 ÷ 20 = **0.6**, so supply is inelastic.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State any three features of perfect competition.", a: "1. A large number of buyers and sellers, so no one can influence the price.\n2. Homogeneous product: all firms sell identical goods.\n3. Free entry and exit of firms, and perfect information about prices (any one more is acceptable). As a result each firm is a price-taker.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A firm sells at a price of ₹12 per unit. Prepare a table of TR, AR and MR for outputs 1 to 5 and state what it shows.", a: "q: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\nTR = p × q: 12, 24, 36, 48, 60\nAR = TR ÷ q: 12, 12, 12, 12, 12\nMR = change in TR: 12, 12, 12, 12, 12\nAR = MR = price at every output. TR is a straight line through the origin, and the AR (= MR) curve is a horizontal line at ₹12.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A firm's minimum AVC is ₹8 and its minimum average cost is ₹11. What will the firm do in the short run if the price is (a) ₹6, (b) ₹9, (c) ₹11?", a: "(a) ₹6 is below min AVC: it cannot cover variable cost, so the firm **shuts down** (loses only TFC).\n(b) ₹9 lies between min AVC (₹8) and min AC (₹11): the firm makes a loss but **continues production** since it covers all variable cost and part of fixed cost.\n(c) ₹11 equals min AC: this is the **break-even point**, the firm earns only normal profit.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A competitive firm faces a price of ₹18 and has TFC of ₹30. Its total variable cost for 1 to 6 units is ₹14, ₹24, ₹32, ₹42, ₹56 and ₹76. Find (a) MC at each output, (b) the profit-maximising output, (c) the profit, (d) whether the firm earns super-normal profit.", a: "TC = TVC + 30 = 44, 54, 62, 72, 86, 106.\n(a) MC = change in TC (TC at zero = 30): 14, 10, 8, 10, 14, 20.\n(b) Compare price (= MR = ₹18) with MC. The 5th unit adds ₹18 to revenue and ₹14 to cost (profit rises), the 6th adds ₹18 and ₹20 (profit falls). So output = **5 units** (MC rising).\n(c) Profit = TR − TC = 90 − 86 = **₹4**. (Profit at 4 units is 0; at 6 units it is ₹2.)\n(d) AC at 5 units = 86 ÷ 5 = ₹17.20 < price ₹18, so the firm earns **super-normal profit** (₹0.80 per unit × 5 = ₹4).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain price elasticity of supply and find it for each of the following straight-line supply curves.\n(a) Q = 3p at p = ₹6\n(b) Q = 10 + 2p at p = ₹5\n(c) Q = 2p − 6 at p = ₹5", a: "Price elasticity of supply eS = (% change in quantity supplied) ÷ (% change in price) = (ΔQ ÷ Δp) × (p ÷ Q). It is positive because supply curves slope upward; eS > 1 is elastic, eS < 1 is inelastic and eS = 1 is unitary.\n(a) At p = 6, Q = 18. eS = 3 × 6 ÷ 18 = **1**. The curve passes through the origin.\n(b) At p = 5, Q = 20. eS = 2 × 5 ÷ 20 = **0.5** (< 1). The curve meets the quantity axis at a positive value (10), so supply is inelastic.\n(c) At p = 5, Q = 4. eS = 2 × 5 ÷ 4 = **2.5** (> 1). The curve meets the price axis at a positive value (p = 3), so supply is elastic.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Mohan runs a brick kiln in a perfectly competitive market. The price is ₹8 per brick. He produces 1,000 bricks a month at a total fixed cost of ₹2,000 and a total variable cost of ₹5,200.\n(i) Find his profit.\n(ii) Find AVC and AC per brick.\n(iii) If the price falls to ₹4.50 (costs unchanged), should he continue to produce? Give a reason.", a: "(i) TR = 8 × 1,000 = ₹8,000; TC = 2,000 + 5,200 = ₹7,200. Profit = **₹800**.\n(ii) AVC = 5,200 ÷ 1,000 = **₹5.20**; AC = 7,200 ÷ 1,000 = **₹7.20**.\n(iii) At ₹4.50 the price is below AVC (₹5.20), so TR (₹4,500) does not cover even the variable cost (₹5,200). If he produces, the loss is 7,200 − 4,500 = ₹2,700; if he shuts down, he loses only the fixed cost ₹2,000. So he should **shut down**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "market-equilibrium": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A market is in equilibrium when:", options: ["the price is as low as possible", "the government fixes the price", "the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied", "the quantity supplied is greater than the quantity demanded"], answer: 2, explain: "At the equilibrium price qD = qS, so there is neither excess demand nor excess supply and no pressure on the price to change.", level: 1 },
      { q: "At a price above the equilibrium price, there is:", options: ["excess supply", "excess demand", "equilibrium", "a shortage"], answer: 0, explain: "A higher price raises quantity supplied and lowers quantity demanded, so qS > qD: excess supply. Sellers compete and the price falls.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The minimum wage fixed by the government is an example of a:", options: ["price ceiling", "market clearing price", "subsidy", "price floor"], answer: 3, explain: "A minimum wage is a lower limit on the wage, fixed above the equilibrium wage, so it is a price floor.", level: 1 },
      { q: "If supply increases and demand is unchanged (fixed number of firms), the equilibrium price:", options: ["rises and quantity falls", "falls and quantity rises", "rises and quantity rises", "falls and quantity falls"], answer: 1, explain: "A rightward shift of supply along an unchanged demand curve lowers the price and raises the quantity.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A perfectly competitive firm hires labour up to the point where the wage equals:", options: ["the average product of labour", "the marginal cost of output", "the value of the marginal product of labour (p × MPL)", "the total revenue"], answer: 2, explain: "The firm hires labour until w = VMPL = p × MPL. Hiring beyond this point adds more to cost than to revenue.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The market demand is qD = 120 − 2p and the market supply is qS = 20 + 3p. The equilibrium price is:", options: ["₹10", "₹20", "₹25", "₹30"], answer: 1, explain: "120 − 2p = 20 + 3p gives 100 = 5p, so p* = ₹20 (and q* = 80 units).", level: 2 },
      { q: "For the market with qD = 120 − 2p and qS = 20 + 3p, what is the excess demand at a price of ₹10?", options: ["30 units", "40 units", "50 units", "100 units"], answer: 2, explain: "At p = 10: qD = 120 − 20 = 100 and qS = 20 + 30 = 50. Excess demand = 100 − 50 = 50 units, so the price will rise.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In a market with free entry and exit of firms, an increase in demand raises the equilibrium price permanently.\nReason (R): New firms enter when existing firms earn super-normal profit, which pushes the price back to the minimum average cost.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "With free entry and exit the long-run price returns to the minimum AC after demand rises; only the quantity and the number of firms increase. So A is false and R is true.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: The government fixes the price of a cooking-gas cylinder below the equilibrium price. At this price consumers want 120 cylinders a week but suppliers offer only 90. This will result in:", options: ["a surplus of 30 cylinders and falling prices", "no change in the market", "an excess supply, which the government buys", "a shortage of 30 cylinders, queues and possible black marketing"], answer: 3, explain: "A price ceiling below p* makes qD > qS. The shortage of 30 cylinders leads to queues, rationing and black markets.", level: 2 },
      { q: "If both demand and supply of a good increase (fixed number of firms), the equilibrium quantity will:", options: ["rise, while the price may rise, fall or remain unchanged", "fall and the price will rise", "remain the same and the price will rise", "rise and the price will certainly rise"], answer: 0, explain: "Both shifts raise the quantity. The effect on price depends on which shift is larger: larger demand shift raises it, larger supply shift lowers it, equal shifts leave it unchanged.", level: 2 },

      { q: "In a perfectly competitive market with free entry and exit, every firm has a minimum LRAC of ₹25 at an output of 10 units. The market demand is qD = 600 − 8p. The number of firms in equilibrium is:", options: ["40", "60", "20", "25"], answer: 0, explain: "With free entry and exit p* = min AC = ₹25. Market quantity = 600 − 8 × 25 = 400. Number of firms = 400 ÷ 10 = 40.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The market demand is qD = 200 − 2p and the market supply is qS = 60 + 5p. The government fixes a minimum price of ₹25 and buys all the surplus at that price. The government's expenditure is:", options: ["₹700", "₹4,625", "₹3,750", "₹875"], answer: 3, explain: "p* = ₹20 (200 − 2p = 60 + 5p gives p = 20). At ₹25: qD = 150 and qS = 185, so surplus = 35 units. Expenditure = 35 × 25 = ₹875.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A price floor set above the equilibrium price leads to excess supply.\nReason (R): At a price above the equilibrium price the quantity supplied exceeds the quantity demanded.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "A binding floor keeps the price above p*, where qS > qD. That surplus is the excess supply, so R explains A.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A firm sells its product at ₹10 per unit in a competitive market. The marginal products of its 5th, 6th, 7th and 8th workers are 9, 7, 5 and 4 units. The daily wage is ₹55. The number of workers the firm will hire is:", options: ["5", "7", "6", "8"], answer: 2, explain: "VMPL = 10 × MPL = ₹90, ₹70, ₹50, ₹40 for the 5th to 8th worker. The firm hires while VMPL ≥ wage (₹55): the 6th worker (₹70) is worth hiring, the 7th (₹50) is not. So 6 workers.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define equilibrium price.", a: "The equilibrium price is the price at which the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied (market demand = market supply), so there is no tendency for the price to change.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is excess demand?", a: "Excess demand is the situation in which the quantity demanded exceeds the quantity supplied at the existing price (qD > qS). It occurs when the price is below the equilibrium price.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "The market demand is qD = 80 − p and the market supply is qS = 20 + 2p. Find the equilibrium price and quantity.", a: "Set qD = qS: 80 − p = 20 + 2p, so 60 = 3p and p* = **₹20**.\nq* = 80 − 20 = **60 units** (check: 20 + 2 × 20 = 60).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two problems that arise when the government fixes a price ceiling.", a: "1. It creates **excess demand (a shortage)**, because at the low price the quantity demanded exceeds the quantity supplied.\n2. Shortages lead to **long queues, rationing and black marketing**, where the good is sold above the ceiling price.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The market demand is qD = 300 − 4p and the market supply is qS = 40 + 6p. Find (a) the equilibrium price and quantity, and (b) the excess supply or demand at p = ₹30.", a: "(a) 300 − 4p = 40 + 6p, so 260 = 10p and p* = **₹26**. q* = 300 − 104 = **196 units** (check: 40 + 156 = 196).\n(b) At p = 30: qD = 300 − 120 = 180 and qS = 40 + 180 = 220. Since qS > qD there is **excess supply of 40 units**, so the price will fall towards ₹26.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "With a fixed number of firms, explain what happens to the equilibrium price and quantity when supply decreases (for example because of a rise in input prices).", a: "1. The supply curve shifts to the left, so at the old price qS < qD: there is excess demand.\n2. Buyers compete for the smaller quantity, so the price rises.\n3. As the price rises, quantity demanded falls and quantity supplied rises along the new supply curve. The new equilibrium has a **higher price and a lower quantity**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how the wage is determined in a perfectly competitive labour market.", a: "1. The demand for labour comes from firms. A firm hires labour until the wage equals the value of the marginal product of labour, w = p × MPL; the labour demand curve slopes downward because MPL diminishes.\n2. The supply of labour comes from households and usually slopes upward: a higher wage brings more workers.\n3. The equilibrium wage is where the demand for labour equals the supply of labour; above it there is unemployment (excess supply of labour) and below it firms compete for labour and raise wages.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "In a perfectly competitive market with free entry and exit, each firm has a minimum LRAC of ₹30 reached at 10 units of output. The market demand is qD = 400 − 5p. Find (a) the equilibrium price, (b) the equilibrium quantity, (c) the number of firms. (d) What happens if demand rises to qD = 500 − 5p?", a: "(a) With free entry and exit, firms earn only normal profit, so p* = min AC = **₹30**.\n(b) q* = 400 − 5 × 30 = **250 units**.\n(c) Number of firms = 250 ÷ 10 = **25**.\n(d) New demand: q* = 500 − 150 = 350 units at the same price ₹30. The extra profit attracts new firms: number of firms = 350 ÷ 10 = **35**. The price stays at min AC (₹30); only quantity and the number of firms increase.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the meaning and consequences of (a) a price ceiling and (b) a price floor, with an example of each.", a: "(a) A **price ceiling** is a maximum price fixed by the government **below** the equilibrium price, usually for essential goods such as wheat, rice, kerosene or sugar, to make them affordable.\n1. At the low price qD > qS, causing a shortage.\n2. The government may ration the good through ration shops; people face long queues and black markets can develop.\n(b) A **price floor** is a minimum price fixed **above** the equilibrium price, for example the MSP for crops or the minimum wage.\n3. At the higher price qS > qD, causing a surplus.\n4. Under MSP the government buys the surplus and keeps it as buffer stock, which has a storage and fiscal cost.\n5. A price floor helps producers (farmers, workers) but may raise cost to consumers; a ceiling helps consumers but discourages supply.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: The table shows the market for a good.\nPrice (₹): 10, 20, 30, 40, 50\nQuantity demanded: 90, 70, 50, 30, 10\nQuantity supplied: 10, 30, 50, 70, 90\n(i) Find the equilibrium price and quantity.\n(ii) What happens at a price of ₹20?\n(iii) The government fixes a minimum price of ₹40. What is the effect?", a: "(i) qD = qS = 50 at ₹30. Equilibrium price = **₹30**, equilibrium quantity = **50 units**.\n(ii) At ₹20: qD = 70 and qS = 30, so there is **excess demand of 40 units**. Buyers compete and the price rises towards ₹30.\n(iii) A minimum price of ₹40 is above equilibrium (a price floor). At ₹40: qD = 30 and qS = 70, so there is **excess supply of 40 units**, which the government must buy or which remains unsold.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
